{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>Eye.</b> Rav Saadia said: We cannot interpret this verse according to its plain sense, for if a man struck his fellow's eye and a third of its light departed — how is it possible that the striker be struck an identical blow without addition or diminution? Perhaps his eye's light will be lost entirely, and the wound, bruise, and lesion will be worse, for if they were in a dangerous place perhaps he will die, and reason does not tolerate this. Ben Zuta said to him: But it is written elsewhere, כאשר יתן מום באדם כן ינתן בו (Leviticus 24:20). The Gaon [Saadia] answered him: We have a bet in place of על, so its meaning is: thus shall his punishment be given upon him. Ben Zuta answered him: כאשר עשה כן יעשה לו (ibid. 19). The Gaon answered: behold, Samson said, כאשר עשו לי כן עשיתי להם (Judges 15:11), and Samson did not take their wives and give them to others, but only repaid them in kind. Ben Zuta answered him: if the striker were a poor man, what would his punishment be? The Gaon answered: if a blind man blinds the eye of a seeing man, what can be done to him? For the poor man may become rich and pay, but the blind man can never pay. And the general principle: we cannot give a complete interpretation according to the Torah's commandment unless we rely on the words of the Sages, for just as we received the Torah from the fathers, so we received the Oral Torah — there is no difference between them. And so the meaning of עין תחת עין will be: his eye is worthy of being in place of his fellow's eye, if he does not pay the ransom." "And the Gaon said that there is a tooth whose punishment is slight if the injured party is a child, for if it is knocked out he will yet grow a replacement. And Scripture mentioned the hand, for it is an instrument of labor, and mentioned the foot, which is worse than the hand, for a man cannot walk on one foot. And in my opinion, Scripture mentioned these limbs according to what commonly occurs, for when a man strikes his fellow, he strikes him in the eye — which faces him, or in the mouth — which faces him — and breaks his teeth, or in the hand — with which he fights or shields his face, or in the foot — when fleeing from him."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:37:10.355243356Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}